# System settings and tools

Before a change, say what it affects, who can make it and how the outcome will be known. System settings and tools can affect more than the screen in front of someone. Keep a diagnostic experiment apart from a command sent to equipment.

This reference pattern covers the system and tools routes registered by Home Assistant frontend **20261028.5**, used by Core **2026.11.1**. The [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html) lists each route. A route mapping names a design owner. It does not show that the native screen has been tested, and it does not allow a test against a real home.

<h2 id="system-capabilities">Choose the contract for the task</h2>

| Task | Keep apart | Related page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| State or template experiment | A displayed state or template result, and what a device actually did | [Entity state, confidence and freshness](entity-state.html) |
| Action or event tool | A requested operation and its target, and the reported result | [Action requests and results](action-results.html) |
| Logs and diagnostics | Recorded events and times, and a proven cause | [History, activity and statistics](history-and-statistics.html) |
| General settings | Settings for the whole instance, and a person's own preferences | [Locale, units and time](locale-units-and-time.html) |
| Analytics, AI and Labs | The enabled feature, where its data goes, whose account it uses and its known limits | [Accounts, permissions and shared settings](accounts-and-permissions.html) |
| Storage and hardware | Reported capacity or settings, and a promise of recovery or reliability | [Backups, restore and recovery copies](backup-and-restore.html) |
| Updates and Repairs | What is available, how far a request has got and what is confirmed done | [Updates, repairs and maintenance](updates-and-repairs.html) |

<h2 id="system-experiments">Explain what an experiment changes</h2>

Keep technical labels and literal values as Home Assistant shows them. Setting a state changes what Home Assistant records for that entity. It does not by itself tell the equipment to change. The change and any event can still start automations, so say so before the action where it matters. A template preview is a calculation. It does not show that its inputs are current or that a later action will work.

Home Assistant opens its tools from **Settings > Tools**. Older instructions call this **Developer tools**. Use the current label when you give a route.

| Tool | What the design must do |
| --- | --- |
| YAML | Separate checking the configuration, reloading and restarting. Name the scope and keep the native confirmation. |
| States | Explain that a state is being set for testing and that automations can react. |
| Actions | Name the actual action and its resolved targets before it runs. |
| Action history | Keep what was recorded apart from a new request to repeat it. |
| Template | Keep the syntax, selection and errors as written. A preview result is a calculation. |
| Events | Separate listening from firing an event, and explain that firing can start automations. |
| Statistics | Keep correcting or deleting apart from read-only viewing. Show units and the time range. |
| Assist | Separate how a sentence was understood from an action that ran. Keep the provider context. |
| Debug and the optional debug console | Keep native availability and permissions. Separate watching from running code. |

The older `service` route is a legacy alias for `action` in the reviewed source. It is not a second action tool.

An action tool can send real commands. Show its exact action, target and parameters and any permission checks that are available. Keep its result apart from what the device later reports. Do not call a live command “Preview”. The examples here never run these tools.

