<h2 id="system-settings">Show the scope and where data goes</h2>

Say whether a setting affects this browser, the signed-in account or the whole instance. Use the native choice and its current value. If a prerequisite has changed, a feature is unavailable or an administrator has restricted a setting, say so without hiding what the setting means.

For analytics, AI and Labs, describe the feature and where its data goes from the actual configuration. Do not promise “private”, “local only” or “AI off” unless the configuration shows it. Enabling a provider, exposing an entity to Assist and turning on an experimental feature are three different choices. Keep the native notices, consent steps and rollback information.

<%= example('system-privacy-choice') %>

<h2 id="system-diagnostics">Show what was observed, and its limits</h2>

Label a log entry's time and level, and do not assume the nearest event caused an outage. Do not let an old retained value look like a new diagnostic result. Explain how to redact a log before it is copied. Never encourage someone to publish credentials, location history or full household data in a routine report.

For storage, give the volume or location, the unit, what total, used and available mean and when it was observed. Keep decimal GB and binary GiB apart. Free space or a count of backup files does not promise recovery. Keep hardware identifiers and connection values in their literal format with clear labels, and give topology diagrams a text alternative.

<%= example('system-storage-status') %>

<h2 id="system-presentation">Use the shared components</h2>

Use [tables](../components/table.html) for values that can be compared, [summary lists](../components/summary-list.html) for named details and [disclosures](../components/accordion.html) for secondary technical explanation. Use code styling only for literal syntax. Keep the shared heading sizes, quiet borders, visible focus and readable body text.

Keep input borders distinct from card dividers. Where a native editor needs a code control, keep line navigation, an accessible name, selection, undo and validation. Do not fake a rich editor with a decorative text box, and do not replace readable diagnostics with coloured dots. Show loading, no results, permission denied, stale data and a failed retrieval as separate states.

<h2 id="system-acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

1. Identify the exact tool and the account permission before testing. Use a disposable fixture for state and event experiments. Do not use a household device as an accessibility fixture.
2. Check that Save, Reload, Restart, Run and a preview label each describe a different real operation. Test failures and unconfirmed responses without discarding a useful draft.
3. Check the instance, account and browser scope. Test installation options that are unsupported and features that vanish when their integration is unloaded.
4. Check units, times, redaction, empty records and stale records. Keep literal identifiers when they are copied.
5. Test by keyboard alone, at 320px, with larger text and in forced colours. Check that errors are tied to their controls and that dialogs return focus.

The examples are local and use invented data. Record real tests with [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html) and the [Compatibility register and native fallback](compatibility-register.html).

